  "body": {
    "id": "prop:canonical-reductions",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If \\(H=0\\), \\(S=1\\), the proxy is the baseline outcome, and \\(m_1(x)=x+\\Delta_0\\) with \\(\\Delta_0\\) equal to the untreated two-period cohort-average mean change, then the functional in \\texttt{lem:identification-fwl} equals the canonical finite-cohort two-period difference-in-differences estimand. If treatment effects are homogeneous across cohorts and there is no carry-over beyond \\(h=0\\), then \\(\\tau_n(z_0)\\) reduces to the common contemporaneous finite-cohort average treatment effect on level wealth.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "H",
      "S",
      "m_s",
      "Delta_DID",
      "tau_n",
      "z_0",
      "h"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:identification-fwl",
      "def:buyhold-level",
      "def:target"
    ],
    "justification": "Named reductions anchor the new compounded-path object to familiar panel estimands.",
    "gap": "The banked predecessor did not provide a canonical DID or homogeneous-panel reduction.",
    "consumer": "Readers can audit the identifying functional against a standard two-period benchmark before using the general event-path result.",
    "proof_tex": "Under the first specialization, \\(S=1\\) and \\(\\sum_s\\pi_s=1\\) imply \\(\\pi_1=1\\).  The identification formula in \\texttt{lem:identification-fwl} and the affine restriction \\(m_1(x)=x+\\Delta_0\\) therefore yield\n\\[\n\\tau_n(z_0)\n=\\mathbb E_P[\\bar B_1^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid z_0,E_0]-m_1(z_{01})\n=\\mathbb E_P[\\bar B_1^{\\mathrm{obs}}\\mid z_0,E_0]-z_{01}-\\Delta_0.\n\\]\nWhen the proxy is the event cohort's baseline outcome, \\(z_{01}\\) is its conditional baseline mean; when \\(\\Delta_0\\) is the untreated cohort-average change, the last display is precisely post-event minus baseline for the treated cohort, less the untreated post-minus-baseline change.  This is the canonical conditional finite-cohort two-period difference-in-differences contrast.\n\nFor the second specialization define the cohort effect\n\\[\n\\tau_s=n_s^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^{n_s}\n\\mathbb E_P[B_{si}(1)-B_{si}(0)\\mid z_0,E_0].\n\\]\nHomogeneity across cohorts says that \\(\\tau_s=\\tau^{\\mathrm{com}}\\) for every \\(s\\).  Thus \\texttt{def:target} and \\(\\sum_s\\pi_s=1\\) give\n\\[\n\\tau_n(z_0)=\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\pi_s\\tau_s\n=\\tau^{\\mathrm{com}}\\sum_{s=1}^{S}\\pi_s\n=\\tau^{\\mathrm{com}}.\n\\]\nThe stipulated absence of carry-over beyond \\(h=0\\) makes this common terminal-level contrast the contemporaneous finite-cohort treatment effect on the buy-and-hold wealth level defined in \\texttt{def:buyhold-level}; the algebra does not manufacture that causal interpretation.",
